From 99217bb2d1cdf1456ba4358a04962ff49cdf1e10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:13:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+, the samples
 taken on the GPU

The statistics of Dolby Vision sent as HDR10+ (dvbridge_dvplus.h) are of the
picture that goes out: the one dvbridge_render_hdr10() draws, R'G'B' in PQ
within [0, 1] in a float texture. One fragment pass writes the samples of a
grid over the place of the picture (960 columns at most, the rows in
proportion: one pixel in 16 of a 3840x2160 picture) into a small RGBA8 target,
each sample the pixel at the middle of its cell as it is (no mean, no filter),
maxRGB as a 16-bit code in two bytes. It is read back into a buffer and taken
when its fence has passed, a frame or more later: nothing waits for the GPU
(four in flight at most). The GL state of the caller is kept, and a sampler of
its own makes the texture complete whatever its filters. OpenGL ES 3.0: no
compute shader.

Tested on zink over lavapipe (dvplus_gl_test.c): the samples against the same
pixels computed on the CPU (407 040 samples, none more than one code off, 594
one code off by the rounding of float32), the caller's state kept, the order;
and on eight pictures of a profile 7 stream rebuilt by dvbridge_render_hdr10():
3 087 360 samples, 2 one code off, and the statistics of the samples within 0
codes of those of every pixel. Cost on the CPU of a 960 x 540 picture into its
histogram: 0.31 to 0.37 ms on the build machine.
